{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/16/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9caf5b84\" lines=\"28-45\">\n<description>\nRecolor every pixel whose color is not one of the two most frequent input colors to orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid contains at least three colors.\n- Orange is absent from the input and is used only as the output replacement color.\n- Exactly two colors occupy the top two frequency ranks; they may tie with each\n  other, but no third color ties them at the cutoff.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve every pixel whose color is one of the two most frequent input colors.\n- Replace every other pixel with orange.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible distinction is not the numeric identity of the colors but their\n  population: the two majority colors remain, while all minority colors merge\n  into the new orange color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a2d730bd\" lines=\"43-56\">\n<description>\nExtend each solid foreground rectangle toward its same-color isolated pixels with capped one-cell paths, clearing each original marker pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground color has exactly one solid rectangular object and one or more isolated same-color marker pixels.\n- Every marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the rectangle bbox and lies outside it.\nRule\n- For each marker, draw a one-cell-wide straight path from the adjacent side of the rectangle through one cell beyond the marker.\n- Add a three-pixel perpendicular cap at the rectangle end and around the marker endpoint.\n- Recolor the original marker pixel to background.\nKey insight\n- The isolated pixels are not copied; they mark the hollow endpoints of capped protrusions grown from their matching rectangle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"99caaf76\" lines=\"56-75\">\n<description>\nMove each marked cross object away from its marker to the grid edge while rotating the colored cross 180 degrees.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each 8-connected foreground object has nine pixels: a 4-pixel marker-color\n  sprite attached to a 5-pixel non-marker colored cross.\n- Each object bbox is either 3x6 or 6x3, so the marker sprite is on exactly\n  one side of the cross.\n\nRule:\n- Move the whole object straight away from its marker side until the object\n  bbox touches the corresponding grid edge.\n- Keep the marker sprite unchanged within the object.\n- Rotate the 3x3 colored cross block by 180 degrees before placing it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker sprite is a directional tail; the cross is the leading payload,\nand only that payload turns around during the slide to the edge.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac0c2ac3\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nFill each centered square ring with the color of the marker at that ring distance.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an odd square grid with a dominant background color and one\nnon-background marker for every Chebyshev distance from the grid center.\nRule\nThe output replaces the background with concentric square rings centered on\nthe middle cell; every cell at distance d from the center takes the color of\nthe unique marker whose distance from the center is d.\nKey insight\nThe scattered markers are not objects to move; their Chebyshev distances\nform a palette for the centered square rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\" lines=\"86-108\">\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a32d8b75\" lines=\"262-275\">\n<description>\nAssemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.\n- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.\n\nRule:\n- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.\n- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).\n- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.\n\nKey insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b5bb5719\" lines=\"17-32\">\n<description>\nFill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has an orange background.\n- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.\n- The first and last top-row foreground pixels define an odd-width span.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the input grid.\n- For row r below the top, fill columns span_left + r through span_right - r.\n- Each filled cell takes the top-row color r columns up-left, flipped red<->grey once per row step.\n\nKey insight:\n- The output is the top-row span propagated down-right along diagonals, while the allowable region shrinks inward from both ends each row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7491f3cf\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nComposite two pattern tiles into a fourth panel using flood-fill from an indicator pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- Four 5×5 panels in one row, border-separated.\n- Panel 0: cue pixels = wall boundary (8-connected, edge-to-edge, through center)\n           + one 8-isolated indicator pixel.\n- Panels 1–2: source patterns. Panel 3: blank (to fill).\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill (4-conn) from indicator, blocked by wall → inside region.\n- Inside → non-bg from panel 1; outside → non-bg from panel 2.\n- Wall → panel 1 if non-bg; panel 2 fallback when wall is 4-connected.\n\nKey insight: The cue panel's isolated indicator and wall partition the tile into inside/outside masks that select between the two source panels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8fff9e47\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nTurn a 4-cell-wide strip of 2x2 blocks into four nested corner-fill patterns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an even-sized rectangle whose shorter dimension is four pixels.\nIt can be partitioned exactly into 2x2 blocks, giving one block for each layer\nof the output quadrants.\n\nRule\nRead the 2x2 blocks in reverse row-major order: bottom-right block first,\nmoving left, then moving upward. For each block, send its top-left,\ntop-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right pixels to the layer sequences of the\nupper-left, upper-right, lower-left, and lower-right output quadrants.\n\nKey insight\nEach input 2x2 block supplies the four colors for one concentric layer, and\nthe layer order is the block order from bottom-right back to top-left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"880c1354\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nRecolor the separated outer regions by rotating their colors one step clockwise around the yellow-orange divider.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid contains a fixed yellow/orange divider structure.\n- All other colors form separated 4-connected monochrome regions around that divider.\n\nRule:\n- Leave the yellow and orange pixels unchanged.\n- Sort the non-divider regions by their centroid angle around the divider centroid in screen-coordinate clockwise order.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
